course: biology
lesson: 369
title: City birds: sparrows, starlings, swifts, crows and peregrines on tall buildings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A city is full of bird homes if you look like a bird: roofs and wall gaps are caves, church towers are cliffs, café tables are feeding grounds and street lights are a second sunset.
explain: Each city bird uses the city its own way: sparrows hop round the crumbs, starlings swirl over the roofs at dusk, swifts scream over the chimneys in summer, crows gather in winter roosts and peregrines hunt pigeons from the cathedral tower.
explain: Stand on a bridge at dusk in winter and watch a cloud of starlings twist like smoke: no bird is in charge, each one just copies the few birds closest to it.
explain: City life changes birds too: some lose their nest holes when old buildings are mended, and many sing higher, louder or at night so they can be heard over the traffic.
fact: House sparrows have lived beside people for about 10,000 years, since the first farms with spilled grain, and nest in gaps in roofs and walls, which many new buildings do not have.
fact: In London sparrow numbers fell by about two-thirds in a few decades, and scientists still argue why.
fact: Sparrows dust-bathe to clean their feathers: dust soaks up extra oil and makes life hard for feather mites, and they also bathe in puddles and sunbathe with wings spread.
fact: Tens of thousands of starlings fly together before roosting in shapes called murmurations; each bird watches about 7 nearest neighbours, and a falcon finds it hard to pick one bird out.
fact: Starlings copy other birds, phones, car alarms and even human words, and Mozart kept a pet starling that sang a tune from his piano music, a little changed.
fact: About 100 starlings were let go in New York's Central Park in 1890 and 1891, and now there are many millions in North America that take nest holes from native birds; the story that a Shakespeare fan did it for the plays is still argued.
fact: Common swifts can stay in the air for about ten months without landing; they eat insects and drink in flight and are thought to sleep while flying high up.
fact: Swifts nest under roof tiles and come back to the same hole after wintering in Africa.
fact: Swifts have tiny feet with all four toes able to point forward for hooking onto walls; their name Apus means without feet, and with long wings they find it hard to take off from flat ground.
fact: In winter crows gather in roosts of hundreds or thousands at night for safety and perhaps to share news of food, and they often gather calling around a dead crow, which scientists think teaches them about danger.
fact: Peregrine falcons nest on tall buildings, bridges and churches that look like cliffs to them, eat mostly city pigeons, and in a hunting dive can go over 300 kilometres an hour, the fastest animal on Earth.
fact: The farm poison DDT, eaten in the bodies of the birds peregrines caught, made peregrine eggshells so thin they cracked, and after it was banned and nest boxes went up, peregrines came back; the female is about a third bigger than the male, the tiercel.
fact: City birds often sing higher, louder or at quieter times like night to be heard over traffic; city great tits sing higher notes than forest ones, and in San Francisco birds sang more softly in the quiet lockdown.
exercise: For about how long have house sparrows lived beside people? | About 10,000 years | About 10 years | About 100 years | About a million years
exercise: Where do house sparrows like to nest? | In gaps in roofs and walls | On the open ground in the middle of a field | Inside car engines only | Underwater
exercise: What happened to London's sparrows in a few decades? | Their numbers fell by about two-thirds | They doubled | They all turned into starlings over a few winters | Nothing changed at all
exercise: Why do sparrows take dust baths? | To clean their feathers and fight feather mites | To keep their beaks sharp | To change the colour of their brown feathers to grey | To find food hidden in the dust
exercise: What is a murmuration? | A huge swirling flock of starlings before roosting | A starling's song | A starling's nest built in a hole in a tall building | A kind of crow roost
exercise: About how many neighbours does each starling in a murmuration watch? | About 7 | Every bird in the flock | Only the leader | None
exercise: Which sounds can starlings copy? | Other birds, phones, car alarms and human words | Only their own song | Nothing, they cannot copy any sound at all | Only dogs barking and cats fighting in the garden at night
exercise: Which famous composer kept a pet starling? | Mozart | Beethoven | Shakespeare | Leonardo da Vinci
exercise: How did starlings reach North America? | About 100 were let go in Central Park, New York, in 1890 and 1891 | They flew across the whole Atlantic Ocean by themselves in one long flight | They came on Columbus's ships | They were always there
exercise: About how long can a common swift stay in the air without landing? | About ten months | About ten minutes | About one day | It cannot fly
exercise: Where do swifts nest in cities? | Under roof tiles, in the same hole each year | On the pavement | In bird boxes put on the ground | In car parks, on top of cars and in lorry engines
exercise: Where do swifts spend the winter? | In Africa | In the Arctic | Under the snow in Europe | On the Moon
exercise: Why does a swift find it hard to take off from flat ground? | It has tiny feet and very long wings | It is far too heavy to lift itself off | It has no wings | It is afraid of the ground and freezes still
exercise: Why do crows gather in huge roosts in winter? | For safety in numbers and perhaps to share news of food | To build one giant nest that all the crows can sleep in | To fight each other | To keep the trees warm
exercise: Why do peregrine falcons nest on tall buildings? | The buildings look like cliffs to them | They like the lifts | They hide from pigeons there | Buildings are warmer than the sky
exercise: How fast can a peregrine go in a hunting dive? | Over 300 kilometres an hour | About 30 kilometres an hour | About as fast as a person walks | Faster than a rocket into space
exercise: What nearly wiped out peregrines in Britain and America? | The farm poison DDT, which made eggshells too thin | Too many tall buildings | Pigeons attacking them | Very cold winters that froze their eggs in the nest
exercise: Why do many city birds sing higher or louder? | To be heard over the traffic | Because city air is thinner | Because they are angry | Because they eat more sugar
test: «found a swift on the pavement, it can't fly off, prob dying» What is the best thing to do? | Ask an adult to call a bird rescue, as swifts cannot take off from flat ground | Leave it there, because it will walk home | Throw it hard into the air from the ground | Give it bread and water and keep it as a pet in a shoebox under your bed
test: Count them: a boy looks at a swift's foot. How many toes, and which way can they point? | 4 toes, all able to point forward | 3 toes pointing forward and 1 back | 2 toes pointing forward | 5 toes like a hand
test: Count them: a peregrine pair has 4 chicks on a cathedral ledge. How many birds on the ledge, and how many legs? | 6 birds and 12 legs | 4 birds and 8 legs | 6 birds and 24 legs | 5 birds and 10 legs
test: If each starling copies only about 7 neighbours, how does the whole cloud turn together? | A turn passes from neighbour to neighbour so fast the flock moves as one | A leader bird shouts orders | They all follow the wind | They agree on a plan before take-off, and each bird remembers its own part
test: «a car alarm's going off but there's no car, wtf» What could it be? | A starling copying the sound | A ghost car that drove away | A crow laughing at you | A sparrow in a dust bath
test: Why would a poison on fields harm a falcon that never ate a plant? | It ate birds that ate the poisoned seeds, so the poison built up in it | Falcons eat grass and seeds in secret when nobody is watching them | The poison blew into its nest as dust only | Falcons drink from farm ponds only
test: In the game Angry Birds, birds are flung from a catapult to fly fast. Which real bird is the fastest animal on Earth? | The peregrine falcon in a hunting dive | The house sparrow on the ground | The starling in a murmuration | The swift taking off from the pavement
test: «sparrows are everywhere, no way they're in trouble» What is true? | In London their numbers fell by about two-thirds, and scientists still argue why | They are the most common bird in the world and their numbers have never fallen | Sparrows are extinct | Sparrows only live in the countryside
test: How could a new house be built to welcome sparrows and swifts? | With small gaps or special nest bricks under the roof | With no gaps anywhere | With glass walls only | With sharp spikes on every ledge and windowsill to keep them safe
test: «all the crows are screaming round a dead crow, are they having a funeral?» What do scientists think? | They may be learning about danger from where the crow died | They are planning to eat it at once | They are singing a sad song for it, just like people do at a funeral | Crows always scream at night for fun
test: Why can a few animals brought to a new place become a huge problem, like starlings in America? | With lots of food and no natural limits they multiply and push out natives | They always die out quickly | They become tame pets | They stop having babies because they miss their old home far away across the sea
test: Why might a robin sing under a streetlight at night in a city? | Night is quieter, so its song can be heard over less traffic | The light makes it think it is winter | Robins cannot sing in daylight | It is trying to wake people up so they come out and feed it
exam: Identify the mechanism by which a murmuration of starlings is coordinated. | Each bird responds to approximately seven of its nearest neighbours | A single leader directs the movements of the flock | The flock follows a route which was determined in advance at the roost | The birds are guided solely by the wind
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the decline and recovery of the peregrine falcon? | The pesticide DDT thinned its eggshells, and after its prohibition the species recovered | The species declined owing to the construction of tall buildings in the cities of Europe | The species declined because pigeons attacked its nests | The species has never declined
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the common swift. | It is capable of remaining airborne for approximately ten months without alighting | It is incapable of flight | It winters beneath the snow in northern Europe | It nests exclusively upon the ground and is unable to fly for more than a few minutes
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which birds in cities frequently sing at a higher pitch or volume. | To be heard above the noise of traffic | Because the air of cities is thinner | Because city birds consume more sugar | Because city birds are deaf
